Text 2: Archaeologist Tomoko Harada contends that the pottery uniformity across Indus Valley sites reflects a decentralized network of artisan guilds sharing techniques through trade. Harada points out that the Indus Valley lacks the monumental administrative architecture, royal inscriptions, and military iconography typically associated with centralized states, suggesting that cultural cohesion arose through commercial exchange rather than political coercion.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Venkatesh attributes the uniformity to centralized political control, whereas Harada attributes it to decentralized commercial networks among artisan communities."},{"key":"B","text":"Venkatesh questions whether the pottery across sites is truly uniform, whereas Harada accepts the uniformity but disputes its chronological range."},{"key":"C","text":"Venkatesh argues the pottery was produced at a single manufacturing center, whereas Harada argues it was imported from regions outside the Indus Valley."},{"key":"D","text":"Venkatesh focuses exclusively on the Roman Empire as her primary evidence, whereas Harada relies solely on architectural analysis from a single Indus site."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The core difference between the two authors is their causal explanation for the same observed pottery uniformity. The option stating that Venkatesh attributes it to centralized political control while Harada attributes it to decentralized commercial networks accurately captures this fundamental interpretive divergence. The option about questioning uniformity and disputing chronology is incorrect because both authors accept the uniformity as fact; neither questions its existence or temporal scope. The option about a single manufacturing center and imports introduces claims neither author makes; Venkatesh discusses standardized production methods, not a single factory, and Harada proposes shared techniques through trade, not importation. The option about Venkatesh focusing exclusively on Rome and Harada on a single site misrepresents both authors; Venkatesh uses Rome as an analogy rather than primary evidence, and Harada discusses the absence of features across the Indus Valley broadly, not at one site.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-09-28"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-09-28","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);









Text 2: Biologist Marcus Howell contends that sauropod gigantism evolved chiefly as a defense against predation. Fossil evidence shows that sauropod prey species consistently increased in size in tandem with their theropod predators, a pattern best explained by an evolutionary arms race rather than by climatic pressures.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By agreeing that thermoregulation drove sauropod gigantism and offering additional supporting evidence."},{"key":"B","text":"By arguing that predator-prey dynamics provide a more compelling explanation for sauropod body size than thermoregulation does."},{"key":"C","text":"By suggesting that sauropods were actually smaller than the fossil record indicates."},{"key":"D","text":"By questioning whether sauropods were truly endothermic animals."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'Text 2''s author argues that predation pressure, not climate, drove sauropod gigantism, directly countering the thermoregulation hypothesis in Text 1. The option stating that Howell would argue predator-prey dynamics provide a more compelling explanation than thermoregulation accurately reflects his position. The option about agreeing with thermoregulation contradicts Text 2, where Howell explicitly favors predation over climatic pressures. The option about sauropods being smaller than the fossil record suggests has no basis in either text. The option about questioning endothermy introduces a topic neither author discusses.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-09-28"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-09-28","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);
















Text 2: Hydrologist Kwame Asante argues that bioswales, while beneficial at the neighborhood scale, cannot address the systemic stormwater challenges facing large metropolitan areas. Asante''s modeling indicates that achieving meaningful flood reduction citywide requires integrated underground cistern networks capable of storing and gradually releasing millions of gallons during major storm events, a capacity that surface-level bioswales cannot match.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"As a promising innovation that should be incorporated into Asante's own cistern-based framework."},{"key":"B","text":"As a fundamentally flawed concept that offers no measurable benefit for stormwater management."},{"key":"C","text":"As a useful but insufficient strategy that lacks the capacity to manage stormwater at a metropolitan scale."},{"key":"D","text":"As an approach that has been rendered unnecessary by recent advances in underground cistern technology."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'Text 2 acknowledges that bioswales are beneficial at the neighborhood scale but argues they cannot handle citywide stormwater challenges, requiring underground cisterns instead. The option describing bioswales as useful but insufficient at the metropolitan scale precisely reflects this nuanced position. The option calling bioswales fundamentally flawed overstates Asante''s critique; he explicitly grants that bioswales are beneficial at a smaller scale. The option about incorporating bioswales into Asante''s framework is unsupported; Text 2 never suggests combining the two approaches. The option about bioswales being rendered unnecessary misrepresents Asante''s argument, which is about scale limitations, not obsolescence due to technological progress.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-09-28"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-09-28","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



